  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kajiado Pastoralists&apos; Livelihoods and Food Security Improvement Project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The pastoralist communities in Torosei and Ewuaso locations of Kajiado districts face high levels of poverty and food insecurity due to frequent and severe droughts and high levels of illiteracy in the community and therefore limited employment opportunities for earning income. Prolonged dry seasons and droughts result in acute water shortages for both humans and livestock, shortage or lack of pasture and low livestock prices due to poor livestock body conditions and to livestock losses and subsequently to food insecurity. As a result of prolonged droughts and water shortages, livestock have to trek for more than 20 kilometres one way to water sources, forcing herders to water their livestock every third or fourth day instead of daily. Women also spend a lot of hours fetching water. The communities rely on relief food during drought periods. 

The project&apos;s long term /strategic goal is to achieve sustainable livelihoods and improved food security among poor pastoralists of Torosei and Ewuaso Kedong locations in Kajiado District. The project components are geared towards disaster risk reduction, improved food security and increased household incomes. The project components include:  
+ Improved water resources management
+ Range land and pasture management through hay conservation and beekeeping
+ Improved livestock husbandry: restocking, animal health, breed improvement.
+ Income generating activities: small businesses, beekeeping for honey production and buying, fattening and selling of steers.</narrative>
  </description>
